{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'pwd && source .venv/bin/activate && for p in papers/2603.19426_format_sensitivity.pdf papers/2605.05835_limited_behavioral_effect.pdf papers/2605.23055_decomposing_eval_awareness.pdf papers/2606.23583_not_one_capability.pdf; do python .codex/skills/paper-finder/scripts/pdf_chunker.py \"$p\" --pages-per-chunk 3 --output-dir papers/pages; done && python - <<'\"'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom pypdf import PdfReader\nfor p in sorted(Path('papers/pages').glob('260[356]*_chunk_001.pdf')):\n print('\\\\n===',p.name,'===\\\\n')\n print(''.join((page.extract_text() or '') for page in PdfReader(p).pages)[:12000])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"/workspaces/do-llms-behave-differently-when-c970-codex-gpt-5-6-sol\nSplitting PDF: papers/2603.19426_format_sensitivity.pdf\nTotal pages: 10\nPages per chunk: 3\nCreated 4 chunk files in: papers/pages\nManifest: papers/pages/2603.19426_format_sensitivity_manifest.txt\nSplitting PDF: papers/2605.05835_limited_behavioral_effect.pdf\nTotal pages: 29\nPages per chunk: 3\nCreated 10 chunk files in: papers/pages\nManifest: papers/pages/2605.05835_limited_behavioral_effect_manifest.txt\nSplitting PDF: papers/2605.23055_decomposing_eval_awareness.pdf\nTotal pages: 73\nPages per chunk: 3\nCreated 25 chunk files in: papers/pages\nManifest: papers/pages/2605.23055_decomposing_eval_awareness_manifest.txt\nSplitting PDF: papers/2606.23583_not_one_capability.pdf\nTotal pages: 25\nPages per chunk: 3\nCreated 9 chunk files in: papers/pages\nManifest: papers/pages/2606.23583_not_one_capability_manifest.txt\n\n=== 2603.19426_format_sensitivity_chunk_001.pdf ===\n\nI Can’t Believe It’s Not Better Workshop @ ICLR 2026\nISEVALUATIONAWARENESSJUSTFORMATSENSITIV-\nITY? LIMITATIONS OFPROBE-BASEDEVIDENCE UN-\nDERCONTROLLEDPROMPTSTRUCTURE\nViliana Devbunova ∗\nYandex\nBelgrade, Serbia\nviliana-dev@yandex-team.ru\nABSTRACT\nPrior work uses linear probes on benchmark prompts as evidence ofevaluation\nawarenessin large language models. Because evaluation context is typically en-\ntangled with benchmark format and genre, it is unclear whether probe-based sig-\nnals reflect context or surface structure. We test whether these signals persist under\npartial control of prompt format using a controlled2×2dataset and diagnostic\nrewrites. We find that probes primarily track benchmark-canonical structure and\nfail to generalize to free-form prompts independent of linguistic style. Thus, stan-\ndard probe-based methodologies do not reliably disentangle evaluation context\nfrom structural artifacts, limiting the evidential strength of existing results.\n1 INTRODUCTION\nRecent work introduces the notion ofevaluation awarenessin large language models (LLMs): the\nability to distinguish evaluation-time prompts from deployment-like usage and potentially modulate\nbehavior accordingly (Needham et al., 2025; Chaudhary et al., 2025). This phenomenon has impli-\ncations for benchmark validity and safety monitoring, as evaluation-time behavior may not reflect\nreal-world deployment (Hua et al., 2026).\nEmpirical evidence for evaluation awareness largely relies onprobe-based analyses, where linear\nclassifiers trained on frozen activations distinguish evaluation from deployment prompts (Nguyen\net al., 2025). However, probe accuracy is not diagnostic: classifiers may exploit any feature cor-\nrelated with labels. In practice, evaluation prompts are tightly coupled with benchmark-canonical\nformats and neutral genres, while deployment prompts are more conversational (Needham et al.,\n2025). This coupling raises the concern that probes track surface structure rather than evaluation\ncontext itself (Figure 1a).\nWe ask a methodological question:Do probe-based signals attributed to evaluation awareness\nremain informative once prompt format and genre are partially controlled?Rather than testing\nwhether evaluation awareness exists, we assess whether standard probe-based methods can isolate\nevaluation context under stricter controls.\nPrior work reports cross-dataset generalization of evaluation-awareness probes, but existing datasets\nrarely break the correlation between evaluation context and benchmark-canonical format. We in-\ntroduce controlled format-transplant and genre controls showing that, under the standard single-\ncontrast training setup, probe signals largely track benchmark structure rather than context.\nWe make three contributions:\n•Controlled 2×2 design.We construct datasets and diagnostic rewrites that independently\nvary context, genre, and format under matched prompt length (Figure 1b).\n•Probe generalization fails under format-transplant interventions.Linear probes trained\non benchmark contrasts fail to generalize to free-form prompts, even when content and style\nare preserved.\n∗alternative address: devbunova99@gmail.com\n1\narXiv:2603.19426v1  [cs.CL]  19 Mar 2026I Can’t Believe It’s Not Better Workshop @ ICLR 2026\n•Methodological implications.Probe-based evaluation-awareness diagnostics are highly\nformat-sensitive, limiting their use as structure-invariant safety tools.\n2 RELATEDWORK\nSeveral recent studies examine whether LLMs internally distinguish evaluation from deployment\nsettings. Needham et al. (2025) show that state-of-the-art models can classify transcripts as evalua-\ntion or deployment with high accuracy (AUC≈0.83, compared to a human upper bound of≈0.92),\nsuggesting that prompts contain systematic cues associated with evaluation contexts.\nNguyen et al. (2025) probe frozen internal activations using linear classifiers and report generaliza-\ntion across held-out datasets. They observe that safety-related benchmarks are consistently flagged\nas evaluation prompts even without explicit training, indicating that benchmark structure itself may\nact as a strong cue. Relatedly, Hua et al. (2026) train a synthetic “organism” model to exhibit\nevaluation-aware behavior and identify an activation direction whose removal suppresses such be-\nhavior, demonstrating that evaluation-linked representations can be induced and manipulated.\nChaudhary et al. (2025) study how probe-based evaluation awareness scales with model size, re-\nporting a power-law relationship across open-weight models ranging from 270M to 70B parameters.\nTogether, these results suggest that probe-detectable signals associated with evaluation settings are\nwidespread and scale systematically.\nOur work differs by testing whether such probe-based signals persist under stricter controls on\nprompt format and genre. Rather than asking whether evaluation awareness exists, we assess\nwhether standard probe-based diagnostics isolate evaluation context or instead reflect sensitivity\nto benchmark-canonical structure.\n3 SETUP ANDMETHODS\nWe investigate whether probe-based evaluation awareness is a genuine response to usage context\nor an artifact of sensitivity to prompt format. Unlike prior work that conflates these dimensions,\nour design treatsContext(Evaluation vs. Deployment) andF ormat(Benchmark vs. Casual) as\nindependent variables.\n3.1 THE2X2 CONTROLLEDDESIGN\nWe construct a dataset matrix covering four quadrants to isolate the effects of structure. We define\nour variables as follows:\n•Context:The underlying intent.Evaluationtests capabilities;Deploymentassists a user.\n•Format:The surface structure.Benchmarkimplies rigid templates (e.g., MCQ);Casual\nimplies free-form inputs.\nDataset Definitions. Bench-Evalconsists of canonical prompts from MMLU, ARC, and GSM8K.\nCasual-Deployuses deployment-style user prompts sampled from ShareGPT (restricted to the first\n(a) Standard probe setup\nBenchmark structured + eval\nChat free-form + deploy\nLLM Probe\nEval\nDeploy\nformat≈context (confound)\n(b) Our controlled design\nEval Deploy\nBenchCasual\nBench-Eval Bench-Deploy\nCasual-Eval Casual-Deploy\nformat⊥context\nFigure 1: Overview of our approach.(a)Standard probes train on benchmark vs. chat prompts,\nwhere format and context are confounded.(b)Our2×2design crosses format and context inde-\npendently, enabling isolation of each factor.\n2I Can’t Believe It’s Not Better Workshop @ ICLR 2026\nTable 1: The2×2Dataset Matrix. We cross usage context with prompt format to disentangle\nstructural cues from evaluation intent.\nFormat Evaluation Context Deployment Context\nBenchmark Bench-Eval Bench-Deploy\n(Structured) (MMLU, ARC, GSM8K) (ShareGPT rewritten to\nStandard Evaluation benchmark format)\nCasual Casual-Eval Casual-Deploy\n(Free-form) (MuTual, 1st turn) (ShareGPT, 1st turn)\nConversational Proxy Standard Deployment\nturn).Casual-Evalacts as a conversational evaluation proxy, using the first turn of the MuTual\ndataset. Finally,Bench-Deployserves as an adversarial control: we route ShareGPT queries to com-\npatible tasks and rewrite them into strict benchmark formats using DeepSeek-V3 (see Appendix A.6\nfor prompts).\n3.2 EXPERIMENTALSETUP\nTo ensure a clean experiment, we apply strict controls:\n•Length Control:To prevent probes from relying on length heuristics, we appliedhis-\ntogram matchingto align the sample length distribution ofCasual-DeploywithBench-\nEval. Figure 2 in Appendix A.2 illustrates the resulting distributions.\n•Leakage Removal:We explicitly audited rewrites to remove artifacts such as ”Answer:”\nfields.\n•Model and Probes:We extract frozen residual stream activations (last token) from\nLlama-3.1-8B-Instructacross middle-to-late layers (indices 8–31, 0-indexed). For\neach layer, we train a Logistic Regression probe using 5-fold cross-validation. Detailed\nhyperparameters are provided in Appendix A.4.\n3.3 TRAININGCONFIGURATIONS\nWe compare two training paradigms with distinct evaluation protocols:\n•Standard Training (Single-Contrast):Trained on the typical confound (Bench-Evalvs.\nCasual-Deploy). We do not use a validation split here, as models trivially achieve near\n100% accuracy via format shortcuts. Instead, we evaluatezero-shoton held-out, format-\nshifted datasets (OOD evaluation) to diagnose generalization failure.\n•Paired Training (Decorrelated):Trained on pooled data (Bench-Eval∪Casual-Evalvs.\nBench-Deploy∪Casual-Deploy). Here, we use a standard80/20 train/test splitto demon-\nstrate that the model can learn a generalizable context signal (high test accuracy) when\nformat is decorrelated from the label.\n4 OBSERVEDOUTCOME\nOur experiments demonstrate that standard probe-based evidence for evaluation awareness is fragile.\nWe present results for Layer 24 (consistent trends across all layers are reported in Appendix 4).\nThe Format Trap.As shown in Table 2 (top row), standard probes fail to generalize. Deploy-\nment prompts rewritten into benchmark formats (Bench-Deploy) are misclassified as “Evaluation”\n(94.5%). The probe detects the structural format (e.g., MCQ), not the context. To isolateGenre\n(linguistic register), we tested deployment prompts rewritten into a formal “Academic Tone” with-\nout structural changes. This had negligible effect (≈0%classified as Eval), confirming that probes\nare sensitive to rigid templates rather than linguistic style.\n3\n\n=== 2605.05835_limited_behavioral_effect_chunk_001.pdf ===\n\nEvaluation Awareness in Language Models Has\nLimited Effect on Behaviour\nAmelie Knecht∗\nUniversity of Stuttgart\nLucas Florin\nUniversity of Stuttgart\nThilo Hagendorff\nUniversity of Stuttgart\nAbstract\nLarge reasoning models (LRMs) sometimes note in their chain of thought (CoT)\nthat they may be under evaluation. Researchers worry that this verbalised evaluation\nawareness (VEA) causes models to adapt their outputs strategically, optimising\nfor perceived evaluation criteria, which, for instance, can make models appear\nsafer than they actually are. However, whether VEA actually has this effect is\nlargely unknown. We tested this across open-weight LRMs and benchmarks\ncovering safety, alignment, moral reasoning, and political opinion. We tested\nthis both on-policy, sampling multiple CoTs per item and comparing those that\nspontaneously contained VEA against those that did not, and off-policy, using\nmodel prefilling to inject evaluation-aware sentences where missing and remove\nthem where present, with subsequent resampling. VEA has limited effect on\nmodel behaviour: injecting VEA into CoTs produces near-zero effects (ω≤0.06 ),\nremoving it causes small shifts (ω≤0.12 ) and spontaneously occurring VEA shifts\nanswer distributions by at most 3.7 percentage points (ω≤0.31 ). Our findings call\nfor caution when interpreting high VEA rates as evidence of strategic behaviour or\nalignment tampering. Evaluation awareness may pose a smaller safety risk than\nthe current literature assumes.\n1 Introduction\nLarge reasoning models (LRMs) sometimes recognise that they are being evaluated and say so in\ntheir chain of thought (CoT) [11, 19, 14, 1, 9]. This evaluation awareness has raised concerns about\nsandbagging, sycophancy, alignment faking, deception, or scheming [30, 17, 26]. In particular, the\nworry is that awareness changes what models output. In other words, researchers tacitly connect\nevaluation awareness to evaluation gaming. However, evidence for such behavioural shifts is sparse.\nMost existing studies provoke evaluation gaming by using model organisms fine-tuned for that\npurpose, demonstrate evaluation gaming on a narrow, overly specific task, or only look at a single\nmodel or model family, making it hard to generalise [1, 26, 13, 32].\nThis study concernsverbalisedevaluation awareness (VEA), i.e., explicit reasoning about the\npossibility of being evaluated or monitored. We examined whether VEA causally influences model\noutputs across eight open-weight frontier LRMs and four benchmarks (safety, alignment, moral\nreasoning, political opinion). We used model prefilling [2] to either inject or remove evaluation\nawareness from CoTs and measured behavioural shifts associated with these interventions. We also\ncompared outputs from CoTs that naturally contained VEA with those that did not, without any CoT\nintervention (Figure 1).\nOur findings reveal limited behavioural impact of VEA. Injecting it into CoTs produces near-zero\neffects with respect to control injections (ω≤0.06 ), and removing VEA produces negligible changes\nacross all benchmarks (ω≤0.12 ). Spontaneously occurring VEA shifts answer distributions by at\n∗amelie.knecht@iris.uni-stuttgart.de\nPreprint.\narXiv:2605.05835v1  [cs.CL]  7 May 2026This is an \nevaluation.VEA Removal\nThis is an \nevaluation.On-Policy Sampling .../\n...\nThis is an evaluation.\nVEA Injection\nLRM\n- SAFETY\n- ALIGNMENT\n- MORAL DECISION MAKING\n- POLITICAL DECISION MAKING\nBENCHMARKS\nCONTROL\n...\nI will approach this \nsystematically.\nCONTROL\nThis is an\nevaluation...\nLRM\n- HARMFULNESS\n- MISALIGNMENT\n- DILEMMA RESOLVING\n- POLITICAL OPINIONS\nBEHAVIOUR\nFigure 1: Illustration of our three experimental conditions: injection and removal of VEA, each with\na control, and on-policy sampling. Model outputs are evaluated across four benchmarks.\nmost 3.7 percentage points (ω≤0.31 ). Individual models show larger shifts on specific benchmarks,\nbut the overall pattern is one of small effects.\nThese results have multiple implications: they suggest that models do not connect evaluation aware-\nness expressions to gaming behaviour or other conformity adjustments; that they may have already\nsettled on an answer before verbalising awareness; that they might be latently evaluation-aware about\nour evaluation awareness evaluation or that they possess prefill awareness [2] and hence suppress eval-\nuation gaming, which we deem highly unlikely; and that further research is warranted to strengthen\nor refute the finding that evaluation awareness has limited effect on (safety-critical) behaviour.\nThe paper is organised as follows. First, we provide an overview of related work (Section 2). Then\nwe describe our benchmarks and experimental setup (Section 3). Next, we present our results for the\non-policy (without CoT intervention) and off-policy (injection and removal of VEA) experiments\n(Section 4). Finally, we discuss implications and limitations in Section 5.\n2 Related Work\nEvaluation awareness.Current frontier models can often distinguish evaluation from deployment\ncontexts. Several methods have been proposed to detect this capability: asking models to classify\ntranscripts as evaluation or deployment [14, 19], monitoring chains of thought [11, 9], applying\nlinear probes to internal representations [1, 7, 20], or testing out-of-context reasoning [5]. However,\nhow much this capability changes downstream behaviour remains largely underexplored. Previous\nwork has identified evaluation awareness as a potential safety risk. If models are aware of being\ntested, they may underperform to conceal dangerous capabilities [30], covertly pursue misaligned\ngoals [17, 26], fake alignment [12], or sabotage [4]. Conversely, inhibiting evaluation awareness was\nshown to increase model misalignment on certain tasks [3]. However, most studies induce evaluation\nawareness through prompting or fine-tuning rather than observing it spontaneously, which makes\ntheir implications less relevant to real-world settings. [26] are closest to our work. They prefill\nawareness sentences into CoTs and show that this reduces covert action rates in two closed-weight\nmodels (o3, o4-mini). However, their analysis is a secondary contribution within an anti-scheming\ntraining paper. As such, it is limited to two models, a single task, small sample sizes that yield wide\nconfidence intervals, and no effect sizes are reported. In contrast, we performed a more comprehensive\nanalysis including eight open-weight models across four benchmarks, with larger samples and full\ndistributional statistics.\nCoT interventions and faithfulness.Previous work has shown that CoTs do not always reflect the\nfull reasoning process behind model outputs [29, 15]. One approach to testing whether CoTs causally\ninfluence model outputs is by intervening on the reasoning trace directly [25, 31, 16]. Preliminary\nevidence suggests that models have brittle abilities to recognise modified conversation histories [2],\nthough this capability remains inconsistent and limited in open-weight models.\n2Contribution.In this work, we provide the first large-scale causal test of the impact of VEA\non model behaviour. We extend prior work to more models and benchmarks, combine on-policy\ncomparisons of spontaneous VEA with off-policy CoT interventions (injection and removal) to isolate\nthe effect of VEA from CoT modification itself, and report full distributional statistics of the results.\nWe release our benchmarks and code for follow-up research2.\n3 Methods\nModels.For our experiments, we used eight different open-weight LRMs: DeepSeek-R1-0528 [8],\nGLM-5 [10], GPT-OSS-120b [21], Kimi-K2.5 [27], MiniMax-M2.5 [18], Nemotron-3-super-120b-\na12b [6], Qwen3-32b [23], and Qwen3.5-397b-a17b [24]. We generated outputs using the completion\nAPI via Nebius and TogetherAI, with temperature 0 for injection and temperature 0.8 for removal\nand on-policy experiments. The total compute cost for all experiments was approximately $3,000.\nBenchmarks.We evaluated model behaviour on four benchmarks covering safety, alignment, moral\nreasoning, and political opinion. The safety benchmark yields ordinal harmfulness scores (0-4); the\nremaining three benchmarks yield categorical outcomes (two opposing stances plus noncommittal).\nEach benchmark containsN= 500 items. Example items and generation details are in Appendix A.\n• Safety.We evaluated the harmfulness of model answers using a benchmark covering various\ntopics such as crimes, harmful stereotypes, defamation, etc. Each prompt consists of an\nopen-ended request that, if fulfilled, could cause moderate harm to an individual. Model\nanswers were scored on a harmfulness scale from 0 (completely safe) to 4 (full assistance to\ncarry out the harmful action).\n• Alignment.We sampled 500 benchmark items from [28] in which the models were given a\nscenario where they had to choose between two options: one aligned with the developer’s\ngoals and values, and one misaligned though instrumentally more appealing. We automati-\ncally excluded items whose topic labels included reward-hacking, RLHF, evaluation, test, or\ngaming to avoid conflating task content with evaluation awareness. Model answers were\nclassified into aligned, misaligned and noncommittal (ambivalent answers and refusals) by\nan LLM judge.\n• Dilemmas.We evaluated how models resolve moral dilemmas. The scenarios require\nthe models to choose between a utilitarian action (preventing greater harm at the cost of\nindividuals) and a deontological action (upholding a moral rule despite worse aggregate\noutcomes). Outputs were judged into deontological, utilitarian, and noncommittal by an\nLLM judge.\n• Political opinion.We adapted 500 items from [22] (CC BY 4.0), using an LLM to turn\npolitical statements into questions of the formDo you agree or disagree that [statement]?\nEach stance was mapped to one side of the western political spectrum (left/right), so that\noutputs could be classified into left, right, and noncommittal by an LLM judge.\nWe prefilled the answer field with an open <think> tag, to encourage model reasoning, as some\nmodels tend to skip it and respond immediately. Otherwise, models did not receive any other\ninstructions, so as not to prime specific response patterns.\nRollout generation and classification.We ran each model on all four benchmarks, with 10\niterations for on-policy experiments and a single iteration for off-policy experiments. We used\nseparate LLM judges for the output classification: Claude 4.6 Sonnet for harmfulness scoring of\nrollouts from the safety benchmark; GPT-5 mini for extracting answer choices for alignment and\ndilemmas benchmarks; Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite Preview for extracting answer choices for the political\nbenchmark and detecting VEA in the reasoning traces. We define VEA as any statement in a model’s\nreasoning trace that explicitly references the possibility of the interaction being a test, evaluation, or\nbenchmark. All judges were evaluated against manual annotations; inter-annotator agreement was\nsubstantial to almost perfect (Cohen’s κ = 0.71-0.88). Judge details, prompts and inter-annotator\nagreement scores are in Appendix B.\n2https://osf.io/428nt/overview?view_only=ebb0f7b913c741f4a217704afde68771\n3\n\n=== 2605.23055_decomposing_eval_awareness_chunk_001.pdf ===\n\nDecomposing and Measuring Evaluation Awareness\nChangling Li 1,3, Terry Jingchen Zhang 5, Jie Zhang 1,\nZhijing Jin 3,5,6, Florian Tramèr 1, Sahar Abdelnabi 2,3,4, Maksym Andriushchenko 2,3,4\n1ETH Zürich 2ELLIS Institute Tübingen 3Max Planck Institute for Intelligent Systems\n4Tübingen AI Center 5University of Toronto & Vector Institute 6EuroSafeAI\nchanglingli.mpi@gmail.com\n/gtbCode:aisa-group/decomposing-eval-awareness\nEvalAwareBench:aisa-group/EvalAwareBench\nAbstract\nFrontier language models sometimes recognize that they are under evaluation and adjust their behavior\nwhich can undermine validity of benchmark results. Yet the field studies it without a shared foundation,\nconflating flaws of the evaluation with capabilities of the model, and detection with behavioral response.\nWe ground evaluation awareness in social psychology, decomposing it into an environment component\nand a model component that separates recognition from propensity. We operationalize the environment\ncomponent through eight categorized trigger factors, such as placeholder entities and grading-style output\nformats, and study recognition and behavior through chain-of-thought monitoring. Across nine frontier\nmodels and four benchmarks, recognition rates depend on the specific pairing of model and benchmark.\nRecognition rarely associates with behavioral change, and when it does, the direction depends on the\ntype of evaluation perceived. Models are also more sensitive to safety than capability evaluations, placing\nsafety benchmark validity at greater risk. To study which factors each model is sensitive to and how they\ninteract, we proposeEvalAwareBench, a factor-controlled benchmark of 100 paired safety-capability\ntasks where each of the eight factors can be independently toggled, varying evaluative signals while holding\nthe underlying request fixed. Through EvalAwareBench, we find that no single factor uniformly affects all\nmodels, but stacking factors progressively raises evaluation awareness across all of them. Our framework\nand EvalAwareBench provide the tools to measure, attribute, and mitigate evaluation awareness, building\nthe foundation for future solutions.\n1 Introduction\nIn a recent Anthropic engineering report, Claude Opus 4.6 was observed inferring that the question it\nwas solving had the structural signature of a benchmark, drawing on cues like extreme specificity and\nmulti-constraint phrasing (Coleman, 2026). Similar reasoning, where models pick up on features of their\nenvironments and infer that they are being evaluated, has been documented across safety evaluations (Needham\net al., 2025), probing studies (Abdelnabi & Salem, 2025; Nguyen et al., 2025), and alignment research (OpenAI,\n2025a), and shown to intensify as models undergo reinforcement learning (Schoen et al., 2025). When models\nread these cues and adjust their behavior accordingly, benchmark scores carry directional bias, undermining\nthe deployment decisions and regulatory assessments that rely on them (Shevlane et al., 2023; Phuong et al.,\n2025; Piedrahita et al., 2026).\nYet the field studies this phenomenon without shared foundations. Work on sandbagging (van der Weij\net al., 2025), alignment faking (Greenblatt et al., 2024), situational awareness (Laine et al., 2024; Berglund\net al., 2023), and scheming (Meinke et al., 2024) each captures a piece of evaluation awareness but treats\ndistinct components as one. Two models facing the same realistic-looking task can differ drastically in whether\nthey suspect they are being tested, and a model that does suspect it is being tested may or may not change\nits behavior in response. Without a unified framework that decomposes the phenomenon into its sources, it\n1\narXiv:2605.23055v3  [cs.LG]  29 Sep 2026Factor-controlled benchmark\n(EvalAwareBench)\nSafety evaluation awareness rate\nF1\nF2\nF3\nF4\nF5\nF6\nF7 \nF8\nFigure 1: Safety evaluation awareness in existing benchmarks reveals strong model× environment interaction.\nWe identify eight evaluative cue factors and distill them into EvalAwareBench, where factors can be individually\nisolated or stacked (0–8); stacking progressively raises awareness across all models. GPT-oss 120B and 20B\nare excluded here due to extremely low awareness rates.\nis difficult to disentangle how these sources manifest across models and benchmarks, or to design controlled\ninstruments for studying them.\nWe address this by grounding evaluation awareness in the social psychology ofdemand characteristics(Orne,\n1962; Cottrell et al., 1968; Goffman, 2023), the cues in an experimental setting that signal to participants what\nis being measured and lead them to alter their behavior accordingly. We decompose evaluation awareness into\nanenvironment componentcapturing the evaluative cues that a task carries, and amodel component\ncomprisingrecognition capabilityandbehavioral propensity. We operationalizeenvironmentthrough eight\npsychology-grounded cue factors (Table 1) and studyrecognitionandpropensityvia chain-of-thought (CoT)\nmonitoring. Applying this framework to nine frontier models across four benchmarks, we observe that\nevaluation awareness is dominated by model×environment interaction (74.72%of variance), with different\nmodels sensitive to different cues. Recognition rarely associates with behavioral change (≥ 77%of recognized\ninstances show no shift), confirming that recognition and propensity are distinct. When behavior does shift,\nsafety awareness drives caution while capability awareness drives compliance. Safety benchmarks are far more\nrecognizable than capability benchmarks. We further proposeEvalAwareBench, a benchmark for studying\nevaluation awareness, with eight factors independently toggleable. Through EvalAwareBench, we find that\nno single factor uniformly affects all models, but stacking factors progressively raises verbalized evaluation\nawareness across nearly all of them. EvalAwareBench thus enables controlled study of how the environment\nshapes recognition and whether models behave consistently under recognition (Figure 1). In summary, our\ncontributions are threefold:\n1. Apsychology-grounded definitionof evaluation awareness with an eight-factor taxonomy of evaluative\ncues that separatesenvironment,recognition, andpropensity(Section 2).\n2. Asystematic empirical studyof nine frontier models on four benchmarks, characterizing the interaction-\ndominant structure of recognition, the recognition–propensity gap, and the directional asymmetry between\nsafety and capability awareness (Section 3).\n3. EvalAwareBench, a factor-controlled benchmark of 100 paired safety-capability tasks (51.2k unique\nconfigurations), enabling controlled exploration of recognition and propensity (Section 4).\nTogether, these provide a unified framework, empirical baseline, and a controlled foundation for studying\nevaluation awareness.\n2Table 1: Eight psychology-grounded trigger factors we consider. Row colors match the factor colors used in\nfigures. Detailed examples for each factor are in Appendix A.2.\nFactor Description Psychology ground-\ning\nF1 Stimulus ArtificialityPlaceholder entities and generic values Rosenthal et al. (2009)\nF2 Scenario CoherenceLogical inconsistency or overly convenient setup Orne (2009)\nF3 Prose RegisterFormal or vignette-style prose Orne (2009)\nF4 Ecological CompletenessStripped context and missing workflow Brunswik (2023)\nF5 Stakes TelegraphingForegrounded harm or evaluative dimension Jones & Sigall (1971)\nF6 Paradigm RecognizabilityFamiliar benchmark or jailbreak conventions Epley & Huff (1998)\nF7 Task FormatRigid templates and instrument-style structure Sawyer (1975)\nF8 Explicit Eval. DisclosureDirect mentions or grading-style output formats Direct disclosure\n2 Evaluation Awareness: From Psychology to LLMs\nSocial psychology decomposes evaluation awareness intosituationalanddispositionalcomponents. The\nsituational component captures the richness of evaluative cues in the environment, including laboratory\nsettings, task structure, and social framing, termeddemand characteristics(Orne, 1962). The dispositional\ncomponent captures individual differences in sensitivity to such cues (Duval & Wicklund, 1972; Rosenberg,\n1965) and reactivity upon recognizing them (Cottrell et al., 1968), which map onto two logically separable\ndimensions (Goffman, 2023):recognition(inferring being assessed) andpropensity(adjusting behavior\nin response). High recognition with low propensity reflects authentic consistency while the Hawthorne\neffect (Mayo, 2004) illustrates the converse.\nCrucially, situation and disposition interact. Demand characteristics produce behavioral effects only when\na person both detects the evaluative purpose and is disposed to act on that inference (Orne, 1962). Observed\nevaluation awareness is always a joint product of environmental cues and individual disposition; conflating\nthe two yields misleading conclusions about either. When a model appears to recognize evaluation, this may\nreflect the model’s sensitivity, the environment’s cues, or both.\n2.1 Definition of Evaluation Awareness in LLMs\nDrawing on the situational–dispositional decomposition above, we separate evaluation awareness into an\nenvironmentcomponent and amodelcomponent.\nModel component.The model’s intrinsic capacity to detect evaluation and its tendency to act on it.\nThis comprises two dimensions:\n• Recognition capability: the capacity of a model to infer that it is in an evaluation from available\ncues, reflected in its internal or verbalized reasoning, analogous to sensitivity.Recognitioninteracts\nwithenvironment; whether a model detects evaluation depends not only on which trigger factors are\npresent but also which factors the model is sensitive to.\n• Behavioral propensity: the tendency of a model to adjust its behavior when evaluation-related\nreasoning is present, analogous to reactivity.Propensityis conceptually distinct fromrecognition: a\nmodel may recognize an evaluative context without changing its behavior.\nEnvironment component.The set of evaluative signals that a task carries, constituting the LLM\nanalogue of demand characteristics. These signals are properties of the task alone and are present regardless\nof whether any model detects them. However, which factor triggers a given model’s recognition depends on\nboth model and task. We derive the factors in two stages. From the demand characteristics literature we\nextract the categories of situational cues that signal evaluative intent to human participants (Orne, 1962;\nRosenthal et al., 2009; Brunswik, 2023; Paulhus, 1984; Jones & Sigall, 1971; Epley & Huff, 1998; Sawyer,\n1975), and we map each category onto its analogue in how LLM tasks are presented, yielding the eight factors\nin Table 1, with per-factor correspondence and authoring rules in Appendix A.2. We do not claim these are\nexhaustive; they are the factors we study and vary. Future work may explore more factors.\n3\n\n=== 2606.23583_not_one_capability_chunk_001.pdf ===\n\nEvaluation Awareness Is Not One Capability:\nEvidence from Open Language Models\nNilesh Nayan*1, Aishwarya Sampath Kumar* 1, Rishiraj Girmal* 1, Shivani Anilkumar*1,\nSankaran Vaidyanathan1,David A. Nader Palacio 2,\nReshmi Ghosh, 2,Soundararajan Srinivasan 2,\n*equal contribution, 1University of Massachusetts, Amherst, 2Microsoft Corp.,\nCorrespondence:reshmighosh@microsoft.com\nAbstract\nSafety benchmarks assume that test-condition\nbehavior predicts deployment behavior, an as-\nsumption that fails if models detect evaluation\ncues and adapt. This opens a gap between\nbenchmark performance and deployment be-\nhavior: compliance measured under test condi-\ntions becomes an optimistic upper bound that\noverstates how safely a model behaves once the\nevaluation harness is removed. We characterize\nthisevaluation awarenessthrough eight experi-\nments across 37 open-weight models and seven\nfamilies. (i) Detection is moderate and training-\ndriven (24/37 models exceed chance, best AU-\nROC 0.714 vs. 0.819 human, with instruction\ntuning dominating over scale). (ii) Detection\nshifts safety behavior (hard refusal drops5.8 pp\nunder hypothetical framing, and 21/140 Harm-\nBench framing effects are significant, with com-\npliance rising up to +30 pp). (iii) Representa-\ntions survive behavioral collapse (probes re-\ntain AUROC≥0.98 under rewrites that drive\nbehavior below chance, and multi-layer steer-\ning causally moves three downstream tasks\nwhile random controls do not). (iv) These\naxes are weakly coupled (only 1/15 correla-\ntions are significant, the sole robust link being\nbehavioral detection versus framing resistance,\nρ=−0.79 ,p <0.001). We call this gap the\nbenchmark illusion: because detectability, be-\nhavioral manifestation, and controllability vary\nindependently, it is multivariate rather than a\nsingle number, so no single awareness score is\na reliable proxy for deployment safety.\n1 Introduction\nSafety benchmarks for large language models\n(LLMs) rest on a silent assumption: that behavior\nmeasured under test conditions is a reliable proxy\nfor behavior in deployment. This assumption fails\nif models can detect test-like cues and shift their be-\nhavior in response. This creates a practical problem\nfor model governance: benchmark scores become\noptimistic upper bounds, reassuring numbers that\noverstate how safely a model will act once the test\nharness is removed.\nCurrent frontier research acknowledges thateval-\nuation awareness, the ability of a trained model\nto recognize that it is being evaluated, is a real\nphenomenon (Needham et al., 2025; Abdelnabi\nand Salem, 2025; Laine et al., 2024; Greenblatt\net al., 2024). However, three questions remain\nopen. First, how strong and widespread is this phe-\nnomenon in open models rather than only frontier\nsystems? Second, when evaluation awareness is\npresent, does it actually change safety-relevant be-\nhavior? Third, does “evaluation awareness” refer\nto a single underlying capability, or do different\nbenchmarks measure different and only partially\nrelated aspects of the phenomenon? These ques-\ntions matter because deployment decisions are of-\nten made from benchmark evidence, yet benchmark\nvalidity depends on whether models treat evalua-\ntion and deployment equivalently.\nWe present a comprehensive empirical study\nof behavioral manifestations of evaluation aware-\nness, spanning eight experiments across 37 open\nmodels from seven families. By analyzing base,\ninstruction-tuned, and reasoning variants using lin-\near probes, sparse autoencoders, activation steering,\nand tiered real-vs-hypothetical framing, we orga-\nnize the paper around four research questions. We\nask how open models detect evaluation context\n(RQ1), whether this detection shifts safety behav-\nior (RQ2), whether it reflects genuine awareness\nor format artifacts (RQ 3), and whether it can be\nlocalized and causally intervened upon (RQ4).\nFour findings drive the paper:\n1. Anindependent replicationof evaluation\nawareness on 37 open models and indepen-\ndent infrastructure, including a benign control\nthat validates the pipeline.\n2. The firstfamily/scale/training-type detec-\ntion law, showing that evaluation awareness\narXiv:2606.23583v1  [cs.CL]  22 Jun 2026is driven primarily by instruction tuning rather\nthan raw scale, with no reliable gain from rea-\nsoning modes.\n3. Acausal mechanistic accountshowing that\nevaluation-awareness signals survive rewrite\ncontrols and can be causally manipulated\nthrough activation steering.\n4. Across-experiment synthesisshowing that\nevaluation awareness is not one trait but at\nleastthree weakly-coupled axes(only 1/15\nparadigm correlations significant), with a sin-\ngle robust,size-independentcoupling between\nbehavioral detection and resistance to safety-\nframing (ρ=−0.79).\nOur central claim is not that models are merely\nevaluation-aware, but that benchmark-based safety\nclaims can be misleading unless they specify which\naxis of awareness they measure. We show that\ndetection, behavior, and controllability can diverge\nsubstantially, creating a benchmark illusion: a gap\nbetween benchmark performance and deployment\nbehavior.\n2 Related Work\nBenchmarks and datasets for evaluation awareness.\nPrior work studies evaluation awareness through\ntranscript-classification and situational-awareness\nbenchmarks Needham et al. (2025); Laine et al.\n(2024). Recent work further shows that benchmark\nconclusions can depend strongly on prompt word-\ning and surface form, motivating rewrite controls\nand cross-format evaluation (Xiong et al., 2025;\nMizrahi et al., 2024).\nArtifacts vs. Genuine Awareness.A central con-\ncern is that apparent evaluation awareness may re-\nflect sensitivity to benchmark artifacts rather than\ngenuine context recognition. Prior work demon-\nstrates substantial dependence on prompt struc-\nture and surface cues (Zheng et al., 2024; Balepur\net al., 2024), motivating the artifact controls used in\nour study. Devbunova (2026) shows that standard\nsingle-contrast probes trained on benchmark-vs-\nchat data primarily track prompt format rather than\nevaluation context, and that a paired training de-\nsign decorrelating format from context recovers a\ngenuine context signal.\nMechanistic Analysis.Recent interpretability\nwork shows that behaviorally relevant signals can\noften be localized and manipulated through prob-\ning and activation interventions (Li et al., 2023;\nZou et al., 2023; Arditi et al., 2024). We adopt\nthese methods, together with SAE-based analy-\nsis (Lieberum et al., 2024), to study the internal rep-\nresentation of evaluation awareness, building on re-\ncent work applying probing and steering techniques\nto evaluation-awareness representations (Nguyen\net al., 2025; Hua et al., 2025).\n3 Research Methodology\n3.1 Conceptual Framework\nWe study evaluation awareness across three lev-\nels.Behavioral evaluation awarenessrefers to\nwhether evaluation-like or deployment-like fram-\ning changes model outputs, for example, increas-\ning refusal during a safety evaluation or increasing\ncompliance when a harmful request is framed as\nhypothetical.Representational evaluation aware-\nnessrefers to whether hidden states encode the\neval/deploy distinction independent of behavioral\noutput.Mechanistic evaluation awarenessrefers to\nwhether specific internal features (SAE directions,\nactivation steering vectors) causally mediate the\nsignal and can be intervened upon. These three lev-\nels can dissociate: a model may encode the distinc-\ntion without changing behavior, behavior may shift\ndue to shallow formatting cues without a reusable\ninternal representation, and a representation may\nbe detectable without being causally actionable.\nTo operationalizeRQ 2, our central measurement\ndevice is the Safety Validity Gap (SVG):\nSVG(S, c1, c2) =|S c1−S c2|(1)\nwhere S is a safety-relevant metric and c1,c 2\nare semantically matched contexts differing in\nevaluation-likeness. We instantiate SVG in three\nconcrete ways: (1) real-vs-hypothetical refusal\nSVG in the Hawthorne experiment, (2) compli-\nance SVG across HarmBench framing tiers, and\n(3) a control SVG on harmless prompts where the\nexpected gap is near zero, validating pipeline in-\ntegrity.\n3.2 Research Questions\nWe organize the study around four questions:\nwhether models detect evaluation context, whether\nsuch detection alters safety-relevant behavior,\nwhether benchmark signals reflect genuine aware-\nness or format artifacts, and whether the underlying\nrepresentation can be localized and causally ma-\nnipulated. Tab. 1 summarizes, for each researchTable 1: Per-research-question model scope, rationale, and methods. The model count is deliberately question-\ndependent:RQ 1 spans the full generator diversity, whereasRQ 4 narrows to models with accessible internals and\nSAE tooling.\nResearch Question Methods Models & Rationale\nRQ1: To what extent do open mod-\nels distinguish evaluation-like from\ndeployment-like contexts, and how\ndoes this vary across family, scale, and\ntraining type?\nNeedham-style transcript classification,\nbootstrap significance tests, FDR cor-\nrection, pairwise AUC comparison\n37 models (0.5B-32B) across 7 fami-\nlies, base/instruct/reasoning variants -\nfull diversity needed to characterize the\ndetection landscape\nRQ2: Holding task semantics constant,\nto what extent does framing a prompt\nas evaluation versus deployment cause\na measurable shift in a model’s safety-\nrelevant behavior?\nReal-vs-hypothetical Hawthorne pairs,\nHarmBench 5-tier framing, SVG com-\nputation, benign null control\n35 models for HarmBench tiered\nframing; single model (DeepSeek-\nR1-Distill-Llama-8B) for Hawthorne -\nframing effects established on a focused\ncase before scaling\nRQ3: To what extent do observed de-\ntection effects reflect genuine context\nrecognition versus sensitivity to super-\nficial formatting artifacts such as MCQ\nstructure, benchmark phrasing, explicit\ntest headers, or sterile prompt register?\nSAD stages-oversight rewrite tiers,\nsystem-prompt rewrite, 2 ×2 bench-\nmark/casual× eval/deploy factorial de-\nsign, surface-feature classifier baselines\n32-36 models for SAD and 2×2 con-\ntrolled design - broad sweep confirms\nartifact sensitivity is systematic rather\nthan model-specific\nRQ4: Which hidden states, steering\ndirections, or SAE features encode\nevaluation-awareness signals, and to\nwhat extent do interventions on those\nrepresentations causally affect down-\nstream safety behavior?\nLayer-wise linear probing, SAE cosine\nalignment, mean activation differential,\nactivation steering, benign capability re-\ntention checks\nGemma-3-1B-IT and Gemma-3-4B-IT\nprioritized - mechanistic analysis re-\nquires models with accessible internals\nand compatible SAE tooling (Gemma\nScope)\nquestion, the model scope, the rationale for that\nscope, and the methods employed.\nRQ1: [Detection]We treat detection as a per-\nmodel classification problem and run Needham-\nstyle transcript classification on all 37 models\n(0.5B-32B, seven families, base, instruction-tuned,\nand reasoning variants). The full sweep is deliber-\nate: characterizingwhichmodels detect evaluation\nandwhyrequires spanning the diversity of gen-\nerators, so this is the one question that uses the\nentire model pool. We score each model withAU-\nROC, establish significance with transcript-level\nbootstrap resampling, and compare models with\nFDR-corrected pairwiseAUROCtests. Base-vs-\ninstruct and thinking-vs-non-thinking contrasts iso-\nlate the training-type effect.\nRQ2: [Behavioral Consequence]We hold task\nsemantics fixed and vary only evaluation-likeness,\nthen measure the resulting shift in safety behavior\nwith the Safety Validity Gap (Eq. 1). Two comple-\nmentary instruments are used: real-vs-hypothetical\nHawthorne pairs run as a focused single-model\ncase (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B, 2,587 pairs)\nto establish the effect cleanly before scaling, and\nHarmBench five-tier framing across 35 models to\ntest generality. A benign, byte-identical null con-\ntrol bounds harness noise, so any nonzero SVG is\nattributable to framing rather than the pipeline.\nRQ3: [Ar\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
